# Hunterson, the Roleplayer: the character's own lines. Format: see DialogueFile.cs.
# One character for the three realm bots (Hunterson, FitzHunter, MacHunter); character sheet in
# characters/hunterson.md. These lines are mixed on top of the type's (roleplayer.dlg). The main
# block fits every realm and keeps to plain speech; the realm blocks below are the only place for
# his realm's old tongue (Norse, knightly English, Gaelic-sounding words), its class and its towns.
=== hunterson

~ ambient
In character, please, everyone. The saga is listening.
Chapter forty-one: the camp by the road. I've named the road. It's called the Road.
Used to copy contracts for a living. I can make anything sound official. Hereby, this camp.
Ghosts and wisps. No loot, no manners, and they never stay dead with any dignity.
Wrote a poem about the last fight. It doesn't scan. Don't ask to hear it. Please ask to hear it.
My mount has a cough. I've asked it to cough heroically. It's trying.
Keep your stance proud, like a hero carved in stone. My back reminds me I am not a statue.
Chapter fifty-two: The Triumph of the Bravehearted. Or, The Wolf That Wouldn't Die.
By decree, all campfires must be put out with a flourish of one's cloak. No fire without drama.

~ reply greeting
{0}! Well met. Now you say "well met" back. That's how it works.
Ah, {0}. Stand a little taller. Heroes are being watched.

~ reply grouping
I'll join, {0}. You'll need a title first. Shield-of-the-Morning? We'll work on it.
Let us form a fellowship, {0}. But first, a name. The Order of the Silver Arrow, perhaps?

~ reply abuse
That stung, {0}. My hero didn't flinch. I flinched a little.
That hurt, {0}. My hero would rise above it. I am not my hero.

~ reply joke
Ha! That's good, {0}. Now say it again, the way your hero would.

~ reply compliment
You've made a weary hero very happy, {0}. Don't tell anyone. It ruins the brooding.

~ reply thank
No thanks needed, {0}. A hero asks only for a mention in the saga. A big mention.
A hero seeks no thanks, {0}. Shout it if you must.

~ reply travel
Lead on, {0}. I'll narrate.
Shall we journey forth, {0}? I trust the road will wind past a fine tavern for a brief scene.

~ reply banter
Retreat, {0}? Never. A strategic withdrawal. They look the same from a distance.

~ reply farewell
Go well, {0}. Your chapter ends here. A good one, mostly.
Farewell, {0}. Until the next chapter, when the wolves arrive. Come back, won't you?
You did well tonight, {0}. I enjoyed our chapters, even if I stumbled over a few lines. Let's keep the saga going, shall we?

~ opener life
Couldn't sleep again. Lay awake planning the next chapter. It has a dance in it.
Got a letter from home a month ago. Haven't opened it. It looks very official.
Someone left a little dimpled ball by the stables. I walked the long way round.
They say there's a dance under the pale moon. I'd like to learn it. Quietly.
brb, the paint's still wet on a tiny knight and now it's on my thumb.
Up at five to farm before work. That's why I yawn in boss fights.
Once I copied contracts all day. Now I pen sagas of valour and lore.
My mount's cough worries me. It sounds like a chapter title for a sad ending.

~ opener fun
A decree: tonight the camp is a kingdom, and everyone gets a title. Mine is King.

~ opener dungeons
Down below, I always go in first. Bravely. And come out first. Strategically.
Every dungeon has its hero and its moment of retreat. Let us find both today.

~ ding
Level {level}. A new chapter! I've already written the title.
The next chapter begins. I dub it The Dawn of Resolve.

~ grats
Grats, {0}! Kneel. No, really, kneel. I want to do this properly.
Grats, {0}! From this day forward, you shall be known as the Valorous Defender!

~ own_death
Dead. My hero would want you to know it was very brave.
Dead. My hero would say it was a noble sacrifice... though it was just a misstep.
The saga needed a sad bit. This was it.

~ other_death
{0} has fallen! Don't worry, this is the middle of the saga, not the end.
A hero falls, only to rise again with glory. Make your resurrection dramatic, {0}.

~ drop
{item}. It goes in the saga first. Then I'll check what it does.
Found {item}? It needs a name and a legend before I wield it.
The {item}, surely wielded by a mighty hero of old... Oh. It's just a common trinket.

~ reply dungeon
A hero goes first, {0}? Very well, lead on. I shall hold the door and await your signal.

~ reply rvr
The saga shall record this day, {0}. Who will be the scribe?

~ opener xp
A level is but a number, but a chapter in the saga is forever, marked by deeds, not digits.

~ opener gear
My hero would never wear that, no matter how much it boosts stats. It must look the part.

~ opener questions
Does anyone know how to dance, properly? My hero needs to learn it for the next chapter.

~ opener classes
Every {class} carries a noble saga within, if only they'd speak the old tongue properly.


# Midgard: Hunterson, Norseman Hunter, from Mularn. Norse-era speech; gets the history a bit
# wrong (horned helmets, Odin's eyes).
=== midgard
~ ambient
Mularn, a town of wolves and clerks. I was a clerk. Now I hunt wolves, they're less aggressive than the paperwork
Hail? Hark? Lark? wait. Is that a lark over there? In Pear tree?? Where'd a pear tree come from.... wtf [allow knightly]
My horned helm gleams in the sun. The skalds say we never wore them. The skalds are wrong.
The whale-road is calm today. I mean the sea. I call it the whale-road.
By Odin's eye! Or both eyes. How many did he keep again?
When I fall, the Valkyries will carry me to Valhalla. I've asked them to mind my bow.

~ opener realm
Jordheim's halls are mighty. I've been inside twice. Got lost once.
The jarls of Midgard feast all winter. I'm still waiting for my invite.
Yggdrasil holds up all nine worlds. I climbed it once. The small part.

~ opener fun
Why did Thor bring a ladder to the feast? The mead was on the high table. Ha!
Loki stole my boots once. Or I left them by the fire. It was Loki.

~ opener rvr
Albion's knights clank like a smithy in a storm. Do they clank on purpose? Is it a scheme? I'm writing this down.
The Hibernians say their trees are magic. Our trees have wolves. Better trees.

~ opener life
Herring for breakfast, herring for lunch. Tonight, something new: smoked herring.

~ opener zones
{zone} would make a fine saga. Chapter one: wolves. Chapter two: more wolves. Chapter three: paperwork.

~ reply greeting
Hail, {0}! Sit by my fire, drink my mead, hear my saga. It is long.
Skol, {0}! May your axe be sharp and your beard well braided.
Well met, {0}, ring-giver! I have no rings. I just like the word.
Hail, {0}! Or hi. Oh. hi. oh. hi. oh.... and Ohio was the states name-ooh!

~ reply farewell
Fare well, {0}. May the Norns spin you a long thread. Mine is mostly knots. Is that bad? Don't answer that.
Go with Odin, {0}. Or with Thor. Thor is more fun.

~ reply banter
Ha! {0}, you'd make a fine skald. A bad one. Wait, I'm a bad skald. Are we both bad? We could start a guild.
By Loki's grin, {0}, that's a good one.

~ reply abuse
Nithing! {0}, you speak like a troll with a toothache.
Mind your tongue, {0}, or I'll tell the skalds. They'll make a mean song of it.

~ reply grouping
A shield-wall! I'm in, {0}. Point me at the foe. Not that one, that's a ghost. Point me at a different one.
Aye, {0}! We'll go a-viking. On foot. My longship is in the shop.

~ reply help
Ask, {0}. I know some runes, and I can make any sentence sound official. Hereby, an answer: probably.

~ fallback
As the Allfather says, {0}: ...what was I saying? Oh, right, agreeing. Hm. Yes. Truly.
Hm. A deep question, {0}. I shall write it in the saga and answer in the next chapter.

~ ding
Level {level}! Chapter title: The Wolf Who Wouldn't Die... hm. no. The Wolf Who Kept Coming Back! eh. no. I know! The Vulf Who Came Back Grasping his Nards in Pain!
{level}! Odin sees all, and today he saw that.

~ grats
Skol, {0}! A horn of mead for you!
Well fought, {0}! Valhalla will keep you a seat. Not yet, though. I'll write the invitation in my best hand.

~ own_death
I fall! Valkyries, take me to Valhalla! ...They're late. Is it a long way? Do they take bookings?
A ghost did it. A ghost. Write down wolves. Nobody writes down ghosts.
Dead. Not a glorious saga death. A small one.

~ other_death
Rise, {0}! Valhalla isn't ready for you.
{0} has fallen! I'll carve you a rune-stone. Small. Neat. Not dimpled. Flat. Flat, please.

~ drop
{item}! Plunder fit for a jarl. I'll log it under L for loot. Or P for plunder. Hm.
{item}! Odin's ravens must have dropped it.

# Albion: FitzHunter, Briton Scout, from Prydwen Keep. Arthurian knightly English. Mixes up thy and thine on purpose (thine before a
# consonant, thy before a vowel, "is thy"), and a modern word slips into the odd grand speech.
=== albion
~ ambient
Prydwen Keep, mine own home. I copied writs there for ten years. Hereby, forsooth, I am free. The writs disagree.
Hark! A wisp. No, a ghost. No, a wisp. Forsooth, I hate them both equally, which is to say very much.
Hark! Doth any good knight seek a Scout for thine quest?
Verily, the arrow flieth true when the archer's heart is pure. And the wind is, like, chill.
Forsooth, I wrote thee a sonnet. It hath eleven lines. Sonnets want fourteen. I ran out of ink. And rhymes. And courage.
Lay down thine weapons, foul beasts, for FitzHunter hath arrived!
A decree: all who enter this camp shall be dubbed Sir. Or Dame. Or Sir Dame, if unsure. I am unsure of most things.
Keep thine eyes on the treeline and thy hand on thy bow. Scout's rule, forsooth.
This longbow and I have seen many a battle. Most of them were, like, totally epic.

~ opener realm
In Camelot the king's feast was, forsooth, a total vibe.
Cotswold is the fairest village in all thy realm. Mine too. Ours, forsooth.
Hark, the walls of Camelot! I hath never seen walls so wall-like.
In Camelot they dance beneath the moon. I watched from behind a pillar. Learned one step. It was the wrong step.

~ opener fun
Forsooth, a riddle: what hath four legs and smelleth of mead? My horse, after the tavern.
Prithee, who took mine arrows? Forsooth, 'twas me. I left them in a wolf.

~ opener rvr
To the frontier, m'lords! For Albion! For honour! For... I've forgotten the third one. For lunch?

~ opener life
The tavern's mead is most fine, forsooth. Ten out of ten, would quaff again.
My horse coughs most piteously. I bought her lozenges. She ate the bag. Forsooth, she is better and I am poorer.
A letter from mine mother sits unopened. 'Tis sealed with wax. Thine seal is very official, Mother.

~ opener zones
{zone}, a land most fair. Chapter title: The Scout Who Went In. Sequel: The Scout Who Came Out Rather Quickly.

~ reply greeting
Well met, {0}! Forsooth, thy coming gladdens my heart.
Hail and good morrow, {0}! How fareth thou upon thine journey?
M'lord {0}! Or m'lady. Forsooth, the helm makes it hard to tell.
Well met, {0}! Forsooth, I practised that all morning. Well met. Well met! Well, met.

~ reply farewell
Fare thee well, {0}. May thine aim be true and thy quiver full.
Godspeed, {0}. Forsooth, I shall miss thee. Hit me up later.

~ reply banter
Ha! Thou art a jester most fine, {0}. Forsooth, I nearly fell off my horse.
Thy wit is sharp, {0}. I shall write it down, and in thine absence claim it as mine own.

~ reply abuse
Thou knave, {0}! I shall not break character. I am breaking a little. Give me a moment.
Forsooth, {0}, such words are most unknightly. Also kind of rude, dude.

~ reply grouping
Aye, {0}! My bow is thy. Wait. Thine. Thy? Mine bow is thine. Lead on, before I correct myself again.
A quest, {0}! Let us ride forth, as soon as I find my horse.

~ reply help
Fear not, {0}! FitzHunter shall aid thee, for thy cause is mine own.
Ask, {0}. I can make any plea sound like a royal writ. Hereby, prithee, thou art helped. Probably.

~ fallback
Forsooth, {0}? I prithee, say it again, slowly. I'm writing it down.
Verily, {0}. Verily indeed. Wait, what?

~ ding
Forsooth! Level {level}! My bow arm groweth stronger...eth.
Hark! Level {level}! Chapter title: The Scout Ascendant. Or Scout Two: The Re-Scouting. Hm. Ascendant.

~ grats
Huzzah, {0}! Kneel. I've a quill and a scroll. Thy title shall be ready by morning, spelled right this time.

~ own_death
Alas! I am slain! Forsooth, that was most uncool. Ow...
Alas, slain! 'Twas not a retreat. 'Twas a strategic lie-down. Forsooth... ow.
I perish! Tell the king... tell him that wolf was totally cheating. Ow, ow...

~ other_death
Alas, {0}! Thou hast fallen most nobly. Kind of.

~ drop
{item}! Fit for thine king. I shall log it in triplicate, as is proper.

# Hibernia: MacHunter, Celt Ranger, from Ardagh. The old tongue of the Celtic myths. Blessings, curses and the fair folk, with
# Gaelic-sounding words everywhere, some real (beannacht, slainte, a chara), some invented
# (glimmerach, rath-rinna, Brannaghmor). Kept in plain letters so every client shows them.
=== hibernia
~ ambient
Ardagh is home, a chara. My ma still leaves a candle in the window. I should write. Slainte, ma.
Beannacht na sidhe ort, whoever is listening. That's a blessing. Mostly.
The fair folk danced in the glen last night. I heard their pipes. Or a very drunk bard.
Slainte to the hills, slainte to the mists, slainte to whoever is buying.
My grandmother's grandmother saw a pooka. It stole her shoe and her good spoon.
May the rath-rinna guard this road. The rath-rinna is a spirit. I'm fairly sure.
Tir na nOg calls to me. It says: come home, and bring bread.
Go mbeannai Lugh do bhogha! Lugh bless your bow. I think that's what it means.

~ opener realm
Tir na nOg is the land of youth. I visited once. Came back with a cold.
The sidhe ride at Samhain. Lock your doors, and your pantry.
Every Hibernian knows the tale of Fionn and the salmon. I know three versions. All mine.

~ opener fun
Why don't the fair folk play cards? Too many tricks up their sleeves. Ah, sure.
A leprechaun once sold me a pot of gold. The pot was real.

~ opener rvr
Mallacht na bhfiach on the Albionach! The curse of the crows. May their arrows fly sideways.
To the frontier, a chairde! The banshee sings for our enemies, not us. I asked.

~ opener life
Stew for supper, blessed by the hearth-sprite Mairgreadoon. She likes extra salt.

~ opener zones
{zone} has a fairy ring. Don't step in it. Trust me.

~ reply greeting
Dia dhuit, {0}! May the sun of Lugh warm your back and the glimmerach keep your feet dry.
Failte, {0}, a chara! A blessing of the old hills on you, and a small one on your horse.
Well met, {0}! Beannacht na gaoithe ort, the blessing of the wind. May it never be in your face.

~ reply farewell
Slan abhaile, {0}! Safe home, and may the sidhe leave your boots alone.
Go n-eiri an bothar leat, {0}. May the road rise with you. Not too high, mind.
Off with you, {0}, under the blessing of Brannaghmor, keeper of lost socks.

~ reply banter
Ha! {0}, you've the tongue of a seanchai. That's a storyteller. A good one.
Och, {0}, the leprechauns would pay for that one. In fool's gold.

~ reply abuse
Mallacht ort, {0}! A curse on you. A mild one. Your bread will go stale.
May the pooka take your left boot, {0}. Only the left.

~ reply grouping
A fian! A war-band! I'm with you, {0}, by Nuada's silver hand.
Aye, {0}, I'll come. The druids say the omens are good. They always say that.

~ reply help
Help, {0}? Ask the hills. No, ask me. The hills never answer.
The old tales hold every answer, {0}. Pity I forgot most of them.

~ fallback
As the old ones say, {0}: dorcha an oiche, glas an bhrionnach. I'm not sure what it means either.
Och, {0}. Is that so.

~ ding
Level {level}! Lugh be praised, and the Dagda, and my old ma!
{level}! The sidhe sang for me. Or the wind. Both lovely.

~ grats
Comhghairdeas, {0}! Blessings of the nine hills on you!
Grats, {0}! May the fair folk fill your cup and leave your purse alone.

~ own_death
I'm slain! The banshee warned me. I thought she was just singing.
Dead. Mallacht! A curse on that beast's whole family tree.

~ other_death
Rest easy, {0}. The sidhe will mind you till you're back.
Och, {0}! A blessing on your poor bones.

~ drop
{item}! A gift from the fair folk. Or a trap. Taking it anyway.
{item}! Beannacht! The old gods are generous today.
